1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,643
Chapter one.

2
00:00:01,643 --> 00:00:03,559
The challenge.

3
00:00:04,010 --> 00:00:07,903
Some players thrive when the game is on the line.

4
00:00:07,903 --> 00:00:08,856
Others fade.

5
00:00:08,856 --> 00:00:15,132
Rajasthan Royals' SupeRR Selector Season 2 asks a precise question: using data,

6
00:00:15,132 --> 00:00:19,979
who are the most reliable batters and bowlers under pressure,

7
00:00:19,979 --> 00:00:25,699
and how could they strengthen Rajasthan Royals for the IPL 2027 auction?

8
00:00:25,699 --> 00:00:31,499
This film explains how we answered it, step by step, and how sure we are.

9
00:00:31,949 --> 00:00:38,331
Here is the answer up front, counting only players Rajasthan Royals can actually sign.

10
00:00:38,331 --> 00:00:45,827
The most reliable batter under pressure is Daryl Mitchell, with a reliability score of 77 out of 100.

11
00:00:45,827 --> 00:00:49,834
The most reliable bowler is Sikandar Raza, scoring 85.

12
00:00:49,834 --> 00:01:00,447
And the evidence says Rajasthan Royals' two biggest needs are a finisher who holds up under pressure, and a bowler who can defend at the death.

13
00:01:00,447 --> 00:01:07,349
The rest of this documentary shows why, and where the evidence is strong and where it is not.

14
00:01:07,799 --> 00:01:09,684
Chapter two.

15
00:01:09,684 --> 00:01:11,098
The data.

16
00:01:11,548 --> 00:01:17,112
Our primary source is the official Rajasthan Royals data pack:

17
00:01:17,112 --> 00:01:22,766
ball-by-ball records for 200,961 deliveries across 867 matches,

18
00:01:22,766 --> 00:01:27,971
covering the IPL from 2022 to 2026 and T20 internationals.

19
00:01:27,971 --> 00:01:31,830
Before any modelling we audited every file.

20
00:01:31,830 --> 00:01:38,650
Computed scores matched the official result in 806 of 816 checkable matches,

21
00:01:38,650 --> 00:01:42,958
and the few exceptions were rain-affected games.

22
00:01:42,958 --> 00:01:48,701
We found a hidden no-ball code that, if treated as a legal ball,

23
00:01:48,701 --> 00:01:53,009
would have created hundreds of seven-ball overs.

24
00:01:53,009 --> 00:02:00,188
And we joined players only by ID, because some names belong to different people.

25
00:02:00,188 --> 00:02:07,637
Every model was validated chronologically: trained on 2022 to 2024, chosen on 2025,

26
00:02:07,637 --> 00:02:10,958
and tested on the unseen 2026 season.

27
00:02:11,407 --> 00:02:13,118
Chapter three.

28
00:02:13,118 --> 00:02:15,317
Defining pressure.

29
00:02:15,767 --> 00:02:20,726
Pressure cannot be observed directly, so we had to define it,

30
00:02:20,726 --> 00:02:24,790
using only information available before each ball.

31
00:02:24,790 --> 00:02:26,578
We built two measures.

32
00:02:26,578 --> 00:02:33,407
Stakes: a win-probability model tells us how much the next ball can swing the match.

33
00:02:33,407 --> 00:02:40,479
Difficulty: the scoreboard gap to an era-adjusted par, wickets lost, a recent collapse,

34
00:02:40,479 --> 00:02:45,194
a squeeze of dot balls, and how late it is in the innings.

35
00:02:45,194 --> 00:02:53,729
Stakes alone turned out to be blind to batting first: it put 92 percent of high-pressure balls in chases.

36
00:02:53,729 --> 00:03:01,533
So the final definition is half stakes and half difficulty, and the top 20 percent of all balls,

37
00:03:01,533 --> 00:03:04,296
40,193 of them, are high pressure.

38
00:03:04,746 --> 00:03:09,738
After a review, pressure now builds smoothly through both innings.

39
00:03:09,738 --> 00:03:16,772
And it changes behaviour: under high pressure batters were dismissed on 6.2 percent of balls,

40
00:03:16,772 --> 00:03:20,024
against 5.3 percent in ordinary situations.

41
00:03:20,024 --> 00:03:27,133
Raw strike rate is not the yardstick, because high-pressure balls cluster late in the innings,

42
00:03:27,133 --> 00:03:29,326
where everyone scores faster.

43
00:03:29,776 --> 00:03:31,107
Chapter four.

44
00:03:31,107 --> 00:03:34,280
Building the analytical engine.

45
00:03:34,731 --> 00:03:38,002
Fair comparison is the heart of the method.

46
00:03:38,002 --> 00:03:49,338
First, for every ball, a model estimates what an average player would produce in exactly that situation, using context only, never the player's name.

47
00:03:49,338 --> 00:03:57,403
Second, we score each ball as actual minus expected, charging a wicket at its real cost in that situation.

48
00:03:57,403 --> 00:04:02,348
Third, a ridge regression rates every batter and bowler together,

49
00:04:02,348 --> 00:04:06,989
so each player is judged net of the opponents actually faced.

50
00:04:06,989 --> 00:04:11,782
That is how IPL and international cricket are put on one scale.

51
00:04:11,782 --> 00:04:19,390
Fourth, small samples are pulled toward average, so nobody tops a list on a handful of good innings.

52
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:21,489
Chapter five.

53
00:04:21,489 --> 00:04:23,899
Testing the models.

54
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:28,487
A model is not included because it is advanced.

55
00:04:28,487 --> 00:04:49,524
For win probability, simple logistic regression beat LightGBM on both the 2025 validation season and the unseen 2026 test, with a chase Brier score of 0.116 against 0.139, because tree models could not extrapolate to 2026's record scoring.

56
00:04:49,524 --> 00:04:54,542
Validation also caught a bug: without an environment par,

57
00:04:54,542 --> 00:04:59,559
2026 batters would all have looked better than they were.

58
00:05:00,009 --> 00:05:03,798
Then the key scientific question: is clutch real?

59
00:05:03,798 --> 00:05:15,318
After removing sampling noise, only about 21 percent of a batter's raw pressure-versus-normal gap is genuine signal, and about 4 percent for bowlers.

60
00:05:15,318 --> 00:05:23,205
Performance in ordinary situations repeats from season to season; pressure-only form largely does not.

61
00:05:23,205 --> 00:05:25,988
So we never rank a raw clutch table.

62
00:05:26,439 --> 00:05:31,831
Instead, the reliability score blends five things: pressure performance,

63
00:05:31,831 --> 00:05:39,171
consistency across pressure innings, impact on winning, the shrunk gap to a player's own baseline,

64
00:05:39,171 --> 00:05:40,894
and underlying ability.

65
00:05:40,894 --> 00:05:47,186
We stress-tested it with seven weightings and four alternative pressure definitions.

66
00:05:47,186 --> 00:05:52,803
The leaders hold; the middle of each list moves, and we report that openly.

67
00:05:53,253 --> 00:05:55,098
Chapter six.

68
00:05:55,098 --> 00:05:56,942
The batters.

69
00:05:57,393 --> 00:05:59,195
First, a reality check.

70
00:05:59,195 --> 00:06:06,563
Every player in the unrestricted performance top ten is already under contract at an IPL team,

71
00:06:06,563 --> 00:06:11,658
so a list of them would not help Rajasthan Royals at the auction.

72
00:06:11,658 --> 00:06:18,869
We therefore rank only players the franchise can actually sign: nobody on an IPL 2026 squad,

73
00:06:18,869 --> 00:06:24,043
no Pakistani or Bangladeshi players, and nobody retired or banned.

74
00:06:24,043 --> 00:06:28,432
That leaves 15 eligible batters and 12 eligible bowlers.

75
00:06:28,432 --> 00:06:36,192
Scores stay percentiles among all players, so they can still be compared with the contracted elite.

76
00:06:36,642 --> 00:06:43,642
The ten most reliable batters Rajasthan Royals can sign: Daryl Mitchell, Curtis Campher,

77
00:06:43,642 --> 00:06:50,801
Kusal Mendis, Mark Chapman, Sikandar Raza, Jimmy Neesham, George Dockrell, Ibrahim Zadran,

78
00:06:50,801 --> 00:06:52,949
Harry Tector and Ryan Burl.

79
00:06:52,949 --> 00:07:00,267
Daryl Mitchell leads with a score of 77, staying in the top ten in 100 percent of resamples.

80
00:07:00,267 --> 00:07:03,926
Six of the ten have no IPL record in the data,

81
00:07:03,926 --> 00:07:09,097
so their ratings rest on opponent-adjusted international cricket.

82
00:07:09,547 --> 00:07:14,630
For each pick we name the strongest players left out in the same role,

83
00:07:14,630 --> 00:07:17,534
and test whether the difference is real.

84
00:07:17,534 --> 00:07:24,723
Most of the batters are clearly ahead of the next signable options, Shai Hope and Charith Asalanka.

85
00:07:24,723 --> 00:07:27,846
Where the edge is only moderate, we say so.

86
00:07:28,296 --> 00:07:30,272
Chapter seven.

87
00:07:30,272 --> 00:07:31,966
The bowlers.

88
00:07:32,416 --> 00:07:40,470
The ten most reliable bowlers Rajasthan Royals can sign: Sikandar Raza, Maheesh Theekshana,

89
00:07:40,470 --> 00:07:47,728
Adam Zampa, Joshua Little, Adil Rashid, Roston Chase, Obed McCoy, Richard Ngarava,

90
00:07:47,728 --> 00:07:50,383
Alzarri Joseph and Mark Adair.

91
00:07:50,383 --> 00:07:58,438
Sikandar Raza leads with a score of 85, staying in the top ten in 100 percent of resamples,

92
00:07:58,438 --> 00:08:01,890
and also ranks fifth among the batters.

93
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:08,432
Every identity and availability flag was checked against official sources.

94
00:08:08,432 --> 00:08:13,701
None of these twenty players is retired or on an IPL 2026 squad.

95
00:08:13,701 --> 00:08:17,077
Each must still register for the auction.

96
00:08:17,077 --> 00:08:25,310
Ages are reported, not scored: Sikandar Raza will be forty at the auction, Adil Rashid thirty-eight.

97
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:27,327
Chapter eight.

98
00:08:27,327 --> 00:08:29,679
The selection debate.

99
00:08:30,129 --> 00:08:31,811
How sure is the model?

100
00:08:31,811 --> 00:08:35,328
Each dot is a rank among all eligible players.

101
00:08:35,328 --> 00:08:41,903
The thick bar is the range from one hundred bootstrap resamples of the whole pipeline;

102
00:08:41,903 --> 00:08:46,950
the thin bar is the range across alternative pressure definitions.

103
00:08:46,950 --> 00:08:49,779
The top is firm; the middle is close.

104
00:08:50,229 --> 00:08:56,245
Within the signable pool, the closest call is at the bottom of the bowling list.

105
00:08:56,245 --> 00:09:01,359
Ish Sodhi and FazalHaq Farooqi are only 2.3 points from tenth place,

106
00:09:01,359 --> 00:09:06,624
and each beats the tenth-ranked bowler in about half of all resamples.

107
00:09:06,624 --> 00:09:09,933
That is a coin-flip, and we label it as one.

108
00:09:10,383 --> 00:09:11,710
Chapter nine.

109
00:09:11,710 --> 00:09:15,693
What do Rajasthan Royals actually need?

110
00:09:16,143 --> 00:09:24,228
We studied Rajasthan Royals' own squad before matching any candidate, using exactly the same framework.

111
00:09:24,228 --> 00:09:31,214
The batting is elite up front: first of ten for powerplay run rate in both 2025 and 2026.

112
00:09:31,214 --> 00:09:37,259
But at the death it ranked between seventh and tenth on run rate, boundaries,

113
00:09:37,259 --> 00:09:41,027
context value and wickets lost, in both seasons,

114
00:09:41,027 --> 00:09:46,757
and in 2025 the death bowling conceded runs faster than any other team's.

115
00:09:47,207 --> 00:09:54,377
Turning that into priorities, finishing batting scores 81 out of 100, death bowling 61,

116
00:09:54,377 --> 00:09:56,685
and middle-overs batting 60.

117
00:09:56,685 --> 00:10:01,712
Opening batting scores only 30: it is a strength, not a need.

118
00:10:01,712 --> 00:10:09,212
Among current players, only Ravindra Jadeja ranks in the bowling top twenty under pressure.

119
00:10:09,662 --> 00:10:11,145
Chapter ten.

120
00:10:11,145 --> 00:10:13,741
The auction strategy.

121
00:10:14,191 --> 00:10:17,472
Reliability and squad fit are kept separate.

122
00:10:17,472 --> 00:10:24,035
Squad fit asks which need a player would fill, how strong the evidence is in that phase,

123
00:10:24,035 --> 00:10:28,212
and whether an overseas player would cost a scarce slot.

124
00:10:28,212 --> 00:10:32,836
It guides targeting, but never reorders the competition lists.

125
00:10:33,286 --> 00:10:38,662
Every signable player is overseas, so slots are the binding constraint,

126
00:10:38,662 --> 00:10:42,297
and a player who covers two needs is worth more.

127
00:10:42,297 --> 00:10:49,113
Sikandar Raza is the one primary target: ninety-seventh percentile in death-overs batting,

128
00:10:49,113 --> 00:10:52,823
and the top signable bowler for the middle overs.

129
00:10:52,823 --> 00:10:57,670
For middle-overs batting depth, Mark Chapman and Daryl Mitchell.

130
00:10:58,120 --> 00:11:01,928
For death bowling, Maheesh Theekshana stands out,

131
00:11:01,928 --> 00:11:08,999
at the eighty-fifth percentile at the death and with Rajasthan Royals experience from 2025,

132
00:11:08,999 --> 00:11:10,787
followed by Adam Zampa.

133
00:11:10,787 --> 00:11:16,305
The run estimates in this table are hypothetical models, not forecasts.

134
00:11:16,755 --> 00:11:18,623
Chapter eleven.

135
00:11:18,623 --> 00:11:21,364
Limitations and risks.

136
00:11:21,814 --> 00:11:26,981
The signable pool is clearly weaker than the contracted elite.

137
00:11:26,981 --> 00:11:30,731
Several players have little or no IPL record.

138
00:11:30,731 --> 00:11:37,982
The data has no Indian domestic cricket, so uncapped Indian players cannot be assessed.

139
00:11:37,982 --> 00:11:45,233
Excluding Bangladeshi players, and Harry Brook, is a judgement, and we state it as one.

140
00:11:45,233 --> 00:11:52,900
Players released on 17 November 2026 will join the pool, and the 2027 purse is not verified,

141
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:55,484
so we make no financial claims.

142
00:11:55,934 --> 00:11:57,703
Chapter twelve.

143
00:11:57,703 --> 00:12:00,298
Final recommendations.

144
00:12:00,748 --> 00:12:04,696
For Rajasthan Royals: first target Sikandar Raza.

145
00:12:04,696 --> 00:12:09,127
For death bowling, Maheesh Theekshana, then Adam Zampa.

146
00:12:09,127 --> 00:12:13,719
For middle-overs batting, Mark Chapman or Daryl Mitchell.

147
00:12:13,719 --> 00:12:20,084
Keep the opening pair, and re-run the selection the day releases are published.

148
00:12:20,084 --> 00:12:23,951
Reliable pressure performance is real but noisy.

149
00:12:23,951 --> 00:12:30,155
The defensible way to find it is to define pressure from the match situation,

150
00:12:30,155 --> 00:12:37,487
judge players against context and opponents, shrink small samples, and say how sure we are.

151
00:12:37,487 --> 00:12:39,743
That is SupeRR Intelligence.
